VERDICT -- brkdiag
==================
brk IS STARVED, FEEDING IT WORKS, AND THE FOOD BUYS NOTHING.

                          brk s   brk gain    final obj
    stock 60                0.4          0      712,652
    beam,brk 60             2.0          0      605,585
    beam,brk 60 floor=25   34.0     62,222      605,585      <- 17x the seconds, same answer
    beam,brk 120           33.9    110,941      533,289
    beam,brk 240           68.3     75,072      480,916
    tpctl=0 60              0.0          0      712,652      <- identical to stock

THE STARVATION IS REAL AND IT IS SELF-INFLICTED.  Selection is max(gain/spent), so an operator that
returns zero on its first short probe is never chosen again; brk's default floor of 8 s is not
enough to finish a set-packing search, so it books zero and starves.  Raising OGC_BRKFLOOR to 25
breaks that loop exactly as intended -- 0.4 s becomes 34 s and the gain goes 0 -> 62,222.

AND THE FINAL ANSWER DOES NOT MOVE.  605,585 with 2 seconds of brk, 605,585 with 34.  The 62,222 is
credited against the pool incumbent, but the answer returned is the best draw the beam produced, and
brk's repack improves a solution the search reaches anyway.  Seventeen times the budget, no change
in the digit.

THE 120 s AND 240 s GAINS ARE THE CLOCK, NOT THE OPERATOR.  Those cells run OGC_OPS=beam,brk with
four operators switched off, so 533,289 and 480,916 measure what a doubled and quadrupled budget
buys the beam, not what brk contributes.

OGC_TPCTL=0 IS INERT HERE.  The OpenMP conflict-graph build the file names as "where brk's 34-36 s
goes" does unlock, but brk was not called at all in that cell and the total is stock's to the digit.
Whether the parallel build pays has to be asked somewhere brk actually runs.

prob_3 SAYS THIS IS NOT GENERAL.  brk took 0 calls under beam,brk and 4.0 s for zero gain in stock,
and the restricted roster was 3.3% WORSE than stock (4,903,064 against 4,748,401) -- so on that
instance the four operators brk displaces are worth more than brk is.

WHAT IT LEAVES.  brk is not the route to prob_1.  Fourteen approaches have now been tried on this
instance and every one lands back in the same two or three attractors the beam produces; the
operator layer is not what is holding it.
